	<h2>The Editorial Framework</h2>
	<p>
		The <strong>ErrorLedger Editorial Team</strong> enforces a strict, zero-trust framework for compiling our engineering database. We do not aggregate generic news articles or summarize press releases. Our mandate is to extract actionable, structural lessons from production failures by analyzing the lowest-level technical documentation available.
	</p>

	<h2>The Primary Sourcing Hierarchy</h2>
	<p>
		To prevent inaccuracy and speculation, every incident teardown and architectural analysis in our database is compiled using a rigid primary-source authentication hierarchy:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Tier 1 — Official Root Cause Analyses (RCAs):</strong> Post-mortem documents, CVE security advisories, and service health reports issued directly by the affected platforms (e.g., AWS, Cloudflare, Microsoft, GitLab).</li>
		<li><strong>Tier 2 — Code & Commit Forensics:</strong> We analyze open-source GitHub repositories, pull request diffs, and developer mailing lists to trace the origin of software defects and configuration panics.</li>
		<li><strong>Tier 3 — Telemetry Logs:</strong> Public network route announcements (BGP), DNS records, kernel crash dumps, and transaction ledger states.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Our Structural Teardown Process</h2>
	<p>
		We process raw technical data through a defined, three-step editorial pipeline designed to deliver practical outcomes for infrastructure engineers:
	</p>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>Architectural Context:</strong> We first establish the baseline design of the system (e.g., how a specific memory controller or reverse proxy operates under normal conditions).</li>
		<li><strong>The Failure Vector:</strong> We isolate the precise mechanism of failure—stripping away symptoms to expose the core deadlock, memory leak, or un-bounded API call.</li>
		<li><strong>Operational Mitigations:</strong> We extract tangible, copy-pasteable engineering artifacts (diagnostic CLI commands, Prometheus alerting rules, configuration circuit breakers) so our readers can immediately harden their own environments.</li>
	</ol>

	<h2>No "Quick Fixes" or Unverified Code</h2>
	<p>
		Unlike typical programming forums, ErrorLedger does not publish temporary API workarounds or localized configuration hacks. We focus strictly on system-level behaviors—such as BGP routing cascades, out-of-bounds memory accesses in kernel space, or garbage collection deadlocks—to expose core trust boundary vulnerabilities. 
	</p>
